  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify continuous-time input-output systems as linear or nonlinear by applying additivity, homogeneity, and the superposition test to system rules such as derivative, constant-offset, and squaring operations.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time system is an input-output rule, written as $y(t)=T[x(t)]$. For this lesson, classification is an identity-check task: we will test whether the system keeps the right algebra when inputs are added or scaled, rather than trying to guess the answer from the name of the system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1+x_2](t) \\stackrel{?}{=} T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first check is additivity. If a system is linear, then the output for a summed input must match the sum of the separate outputs. We will reuse this check whenever we combine two continuous-time inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[a x](t) \\stackrel{?}{=} a\\,T[x](t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The second check is homogeneity. If we scale the input by a constant $a$, then a linear system must scale the output by the same constant. The simplest symbols to keep in mind are a generic input $x(t)$, a second input $x_2(t)=1$, and a scale factor $a=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[a_1 x_1 + a_2 x_2](t) = a_1 T[x_1](t)+a_2 T[x_2](t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These two checks combine into the superposition test, which is the compact rule we will use throughout the lesson. In the next sections, derivative, constant-offset, and squaring systems will each be classified by applying this same symbolic test to their system rules.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $T[x(t)] = \\frac{d}{dt}x(t)$, let $x_1(t)=t^2$ and $x_2(t)=\\sin t$. Compute $T[x_1+x_2](t)$ and $T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)$, then test homogeneity with $a=3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a derivative rule, so we apply the linearity checks from the introduction to the same operator $T$. Start by forming the summed input $x_1(t)+x_2(t)$, then compare the output of the sum with the sum of the separate outputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t)+x_2(t)=t^2+\\sin t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1+x_2](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(t^2+\\sin t\\bigr)=2t+\\cos t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(t^2)=2t,\\qquad T[x_2](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)=\\cos t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=2t+\\cos t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two expressions match, so the derivative rule passes additivity for this input pair. Now test homogeneity by scaling the same input by $a=3$ and comparing the result to three times the original output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[3x](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(3x(t)\\bigr)=3\\frac{d}{dt}x(t)=3x'(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3T[x](t)=3x'(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The scaled input gives the same scaled output, so homogeneity also holds. Since both additivity and homogeneity pass, the derivative system is linear.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Constant Offset System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $T[x(t)] = x(t)+4$ by evaluating $T[0]$ and comparing $T[2x](t)$ with $2T[x](t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a constant offset in the system rule, so we check whether the added $4$ survives the linearity tests. Start with the zero-input test, since a linear system must send $0$ to $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[0](t)=0+4=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $T[0](t)\\neq 0$, the rule already fails the linear zero-input requirement. We can confirm the same mismatch with homogeneity by scaling the input by $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[2x](t)=2x(t)+4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2T[x](t)=2\\bigl(x(t)+4\\bigr)=2x(t)+8",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two outputs do not match, so homogeneity also fails. The constant offset breaks linearity, and the system is nonlinear.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Squaring System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $T[x(t)] = [x(t)]^2$, test linearity with $x_1(t)=t$ and $x_2(t)=1$, then check scaling with $a=3$ and $x(t)=t+1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a squaring rule, so we expect the output to be the square of the input. To test linearity, first compare the output of a summed input with the sum of the separate outputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t)+x_2(t)=t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1+x_2](t)=[t+1]^2=t^2+2t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=[t]^2+[1]^2=t^2+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These expressions do not match because the square of a sum creates the cross-term $2t$. So additivity fails. Now check homogeneity with a scaled input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[3x](t)=[3x(t)]^2=9x^2(t)=9(t+1)^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3T[x](t)=3[x(t)]^2=3(t+1)^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The scale factor is squared on the left, so homogeneity fails too. Since the squaring rule breaks both linearity checks, the system is nonlinear.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying Mixed System Rules",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $T[x(t)] = \\frac{d}{dt}x(t) + [x(t)]^2$, use $x_1(t)=t$, $x_2(t)=1$, and $a=2$ to test superposition and classify the system.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a mixed rule: one derivative term and one squaring term. The derivative part is linear, so the classification will be decided by whether the square term preserves the superposition test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_1(t)+x_2(t)=t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1+x_2](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(t+1)+[t+1]^2=1+t^2+2t+1=t^2+2t+2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(t)+[t]^2=1+t^2,\\qquad T[x_2](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(1)+[1]^2=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=t^2+2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two sides do not match because the squared sum creates the cross-term $2t$. So additivity fails, even though the derivative part itself behaves linearly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T[2x](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(2x(t)\\bigr)+\\bigl[2x(t)\\bigr]^2=2+4x^2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2T[x](t)=2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}x(t)+[x(t)]^2\\right)=2+2x^2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The scale factor is squared in the nonlinear term, so homogeneity fails too. Since one mismatch is enough to reject linearity, the mixed rule is nonlinear.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify continuous-time input-output systems as linear or nonlinear by applying additivity, homogeneity, and the superposition test to system rules such as derivative, constant-offset, and squaring operations.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Derivative System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $T[x(t)] = \\frac{d}{dt}x(t)$, let $x_1(t)=t^3$ and $x_2(t)=\\cos t$. Which choice correctly summarizes both the additivity and homogeneity checks for this step?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T[x_1+x_2](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(t^3+\\cos t)=3t^2-\\sin t$, and $T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=3t^2-\\sin t$, so additivity holds. Also $T[3x](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(3x(t))=3x'(t)=3(3t^2-\\sin t)=3T[x](t)$, so homogeneity holds.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Both checks fail because $T[x_1+x_2](t)=3t^2+\\sin t$ and $T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=3t^2-\\sin t$, so the sign in differentiation changes the conclusion.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Additivity matches, but homogeneity gives $T[3x](t)=\\frac{1}{3}x'(t)$, so the derivative system is nonlinear.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$T[x_1+x_2](t)$ must be computed as $T[x_1](t)\\,T[x_2](t)=3t^2(-\\sin t)$ while homogeneity is correct, so only one branch is tested.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Because $t^3$ is nonlinear, $T[t^3+\\cos t]$ is not relevant; the system must be nonlinear before checking either identity.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "This is a direct derivative check. Differentiation is linear, so the derivative of the summed input matches the sum of derivatives, and scaling the input by $3$ scales the output by the same factor: $T[3x]=3x'$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For $T[x(t)] = \\frac{d}{dt}x(t)$, choose the option that correctly tests additivity and homogeneity with $x_1(t)=\\sin t$, $x_2(t)=2t$, and $a=-2$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Additivity is checked by $T[x_1+x_2](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t+2t)=\\cos t+2$ and $T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=\\cos t+2$, so it matches. Homogeneity is checked by $T[-2x](t)=\\frac{d}{dt}[-2(\\sin t+2t)]=-2\\cos t-4$ and $-2T[x](t)=-2(\\cos t+2)=-2\\cos t-4$, so it matches.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Additivity fails because $T[x_1+x_2](t)=\\cos t\\cdot 2$ while $T[x_1](t)+T[x_2](t)=\\cos t+2$. The system is therefore nonlinear from the first check alone.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Homogeneity is the only required check here, and $T[-2x](t)=\\frac{1}{2}x'(t)$, so it fails immediately.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Both checks hold if $T[-2x](t)=2T[x](t)$, so the derivative system is homogeneous only under magnitude scaling by $2$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "The correct conclusion is nonlinear because the sum includes a trigonometric term and a polynomial term.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "With this derivative operator, both identities hold for the chosen pair. The sum derivative is linear, and scaling by $-2$ gives $T[-2x(t)]=-2T[x(t)]$, so the system remains linear on this check.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Linear vs. Nonlinear Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Constant Offset System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Classify $T[x(t)] = x(t)+5$ by checking the zero-input condition and the homogeneity check with $a=2$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "First, compute $T[0](t)$ and then compare $T[2x](t)$ to $2T[x](t)$. Which conclusion is correct?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
